# loopback listener inside this pytest process; the push transport in proof 1 is a fake.
#
# Produced by the GC-P21 worker on the integration branch at 25af206 (CPython 3.12.13,
# pytest 9.1.1), from the repo root, with:
#
#   uv run --frozen pytest -q tests/test_gc_p21_alerts_proof.py -vv -rA --no-header \
#     | tee docs/internal/go-live-finish/evidence/GC-P21-alerts.log
#
# Two documented additions to the command in the card body:
#
#   * `-rA` prints each passing test's captured stdout. Without it the log would be three
#     PASSED lines and none of the observed values the card asks to be proven -- the
#     `GC-P21 ... proof:` lines below are what the tests measured, not what they hoped.
#   * `--no-header` drops pytest's banner, which names the developer's absolute checkout
#     path. Nothing else in the output is edited: the sections below this header are the
#     verbatim run output, including the run's own summary line.
#
# The card's step 4 says the demo clock reads 08:00 and expects `now = 2026-06-15T09:05+02:00`
# to be about +3600 s off it. That arithmetic does not hold against the fixture: the demo box
# answers System/time with localTime 2026-06-15T18:05:00+02:00 (demo_device.py's
# _DEMO_LOCAL_TIME) and the site zone is Europe/Bucharest, which is +03:00 in June. So the
# test DERIVES its clock from the live box's own answer -- one real GET through the
# unmodified client, one hour behind it -- and the skew it then measures is exactly +3600 s.
# The card's intent (a one-hour device clock, one warning) is what the proof pins.
#
# Proof 2 stops the demo listener and also drops the client's pooled keep-alive session,
# because a dead box closes every socket it owned and `DemoDevice.stop()` only closes the
# listener. The agent is left with no listener and no live session: every further request
# opens a new connection and is refused, which is the failure shape `AlertPolicy` counts.
#
# This file is read back and pinned by tests/test_gc_p21_evidence.py, so a stale or edited
# copy fails the GATE. Nothing here names a real device, tenant, host or credential.

